title: Turning a bus round
mode: vhdl
chapter: Buses and numbers
check: behaves
---- intro
You can join single bits into a bus with `&`, in any order you like: `a(0) & a(1)` is a two-bit bus with `a(0)` on the left.

Joining all four bits of `a` from the other end turns it round: bit 0 becomes bit 3, and so on.
---- task
Make `y` the bits of `a` in reverse order: for `a = "0001"`, `y = "1000"`.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity reverse4 is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    y : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
  );
end reverse4;

architecture rtl of reverse4 is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity reverse4 is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    y : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
  );
end reverse4;

architecture rtl of reverse4 is
begin
    y <= a(0) & a(1) & a(2) & a(3);
end rtl;
---- hint1
Join the four single bits of `a` with `&`, starting from `a(0)`.
---- hint2
`y <= a(0) & a(1) & a(2) & a(3);`
---- solution
`y <= a(0) & a(1) & a(2) & a(3);` The leftmost thing joined becomes the top bit.
---- done
No gates at all: only wires crossing over. Some chips send bits lowest first and others highest first, and a reversal like this connects them.
